<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lugus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lugus was a god of the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a>, known from inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and elsewhere, sometimes in the plural form Lugoves. His name survives in place names such as Lugdunum, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and he is widely linked with the Irish Lugh and the Welsh Lleu. Many scholars think he was the Gaulish god whom Caesar identified with Mercury.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lugus.jpg" alt="Lugus" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Talskubilos · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PenalbaDeVillastar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religion is known from inscriptions and classical reports; the rock inscriptions of Peñalba de Villastar name the god <b>Lugus</b>, also worshipped by Celts elsewhere in Europe, and Silius Italicus and Aelian report that warriors killed in battle were left to be eaten by vultures, believed to carry them…</blockquote><a href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Taranis was a sky god of thunder, the wheel and the bull; <b>Lugus</b> a god of skill and craft; Sirona and Borvo healers tied to sacred springs.</blockquote><a href="/religion/celtic-religion">Celtic religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Inscriptions record hundreds of divine names, many of them local: <b>Lugus</b>, remembered in the name of Lugdunum, modern Lyon; Epona, the horse goddess, popular in the Roman army; Cernunnos, named on a carved pillar from Paris; and Sulis, goddess of the hot springs at Bath.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>They worshipped many gods, among them the thunder god Taranis, <b>Lugus</b>, a god of skills and crafts, and the horse goddess Epona, held ceremonies in sacred groves and offered treasured things in rivers, wetlands and shafts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Their name may derive from the god <b>Lugus</b> or from a word for raven.</blockquote><a href="/state/lugi">Lugi</a></li></ul></section>
